~ part_1
Manager: At approximately 09:39 UTC yesterday, satellites in the Eastern hemisphere of the Earth were hit with an X-ray blast from outer space.
=> END

~ part_2
Manager: Numerous agencies focused their satellite dishes toward the source of the emission, and within a few hours, picked up a signal from an exoplanet 14 light-years away.
=> END

~ part_3
Manager: That planet is known to sit in the habitable zone of its host star, suitable for water-based life.
	But based on the wobble of the host star, astronomers believe that a rogue gas giant has entered the system and kicked the signaling planet out of orbit.
=> END

~ part_4
Manager: If the source of the signal is a natural phenomenon, then its not something we’ve ever seen before. 
=> END

~ part_5
Manager: If the source of the signal is intelligent life, then this may be the last and only message we get from an already dead civilization...
=> END

~ part_6
Manager: In response to this unprecedented opportunity, space agencies world-wide have assembled hundreds of people to independently analyze and process the signal.
=> END

~ part_7
Manager: People are being divided into teams of 4, with one lead and three analyzers, who will individually report findings to be corroborated with the others.
=> END

~ part_8
Manager: Your first task will be to accurately divide the first signal into a time-series, to accurately apply Fourier transforms and get individual frequencies.
=> END
